{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a94462c24cd515ad","type":"tool_result","content":"16:Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI) 3.6-ea.1 is a comprehensive AI/ML platform deployed on OpenShift Container Platform through the Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). The platform is orchestrated by rhods-operator, which manages the lifecycle of all platform components through a hierarchy of custom resources — DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs. Each major capability (model serving, pipelines, distributed workloads, notebooks, model registry, training, observability, guardrails, and models-as-a-service) is implemented by a dedicated module operator or controller that watches its component CR and reconciles downstream Kubernetes resources using kustomize-based manifest rendering through the shared odh-platform-utilities framework.\n18:The platform ingress architecture is in active transition. The rhods-operator Gateway service controller provisions a central `data-science-gateway` using Gateway API (GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoutes) while maintaining backward compatibility through OpenShift Routes. kube-auth-proxy provides OIDC-authenticated access to the dashboard, and the Gateway controller conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically creating EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected. Models-as-a-Service introduces a parallel ingress path through Kuadrant AuthPolicy enforcement with API key, TokenReview, and optional OIDC JWT authentication at the Gateway level. Model-registry-operator implements dual ingress (Gateway API HTTPRoutes and OpenShift Routes) with kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication on backend pods.\n20:Security follows a defense-in-depth posture: all shipped Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptography through system OpenSSL, operators read the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to align their TLS configuration with platform-wide policy, admission webhooks validate and default CRD resources with fail-closed policies, and per-component RBAC is scoped through dedicated ServiceAccounts and ClusterRoles. Authentication is layered across gateway-level (kube-auth-proxy OIDC, Kuadrant AuthPolicy), pod-level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar), and application-level (Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview) enforcement points.\n50:  odh-observability\n69:**odh-model-controller** serves as the serving control plane companion to kserve, managing 10 reconcilers covering InferenceService, InferenceGraph, LLMInferenceService, and Gateway resources. It provides the model-serving-api REST endpoint and manages Kuadrant AuthPolicies and KEDA autoscaling for the serving layer.\n73:**models-as-a-service** is the multi-tenant model serving gateway, providing OpenAI-compatible API access with Kuadrant-based authentication and subscription governance.\n85:| Kuadrant policy integration | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator | Kuadrant AuthPolicy and TokenRateLimitPolicy resources provide gateway-level authentication, authorization, and rate limiting |\n98:| models-as-a-service | MaaS namespace | maas-api, maas-controller; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n99:| kuadrant-system | Kuadrant infrastructure | Authorino/Kuadrant controllers; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n105:| data-science-gateway (openshift-ingress) | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | HTTPRoutes from models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator, kserve llmisvc | Envoy (via Istio/SAIL or gateway-api controller) | Conditional: EnvoyFilter -> ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy for dashboard; Kuadrant AuthPolicy for MaaS; kube-rbac-proxy sidecar for model-registry | odh-dashboard, models-as-a-service, model-registry, kserve LLMInferenceService | derived |\n106:| OpenShift Routes (various namespaces) | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator, odh-observability, trustyai-service-operator | Route resources per component | OpenShift HAProxy router | TLS termination at router; kube-rbac-proxy at pod level | odh-dashboard, model-registry REST, Prometheus proxies, TrustyAI service | observed |\n107:| maas-default-gateway (openshift-ingress) | models-as-a-service (references in HTTPRoute) | HTTPRoute maas-api-route: /v1/models, /v1/subscriptions, /v1/api-keys, /maas-api/* | Envoy (via gateway controller) | Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy: API key + TokenReview + optional OIDC JWT -> OPA authorization | maas-api backend (port 8443) | observed |\n113:| data-science-gateway | openshift-ingress / platform GatewayClass | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | odh-dashboard (via kube-auth-proxy), models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, kserve llmisvc, odh-model-controller | Per-route: EnvoyFilter ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy (dashboard), Kuadrant AuthPolicy (MaaS), kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (model-registry) | central/shared | derived |\n114:| maas-default-gateway | openshift-ingress | models-as-a-service deployment manifest | maas-api | Kuadrant/Authorino: API key extraction (sk-oai-*) + K8s TokenReview + optional OIDC JWT; OPA Rego authorization for subscription/group/binding | capability-specific | observed |\n122:The Models-as-a-Service subsystem introduces a third ingress pattern: it references a `maas-default-gateway` in its HTTPRoute manifest and layers Kuadrant/Authorino authentication at the gateway level. The MaaSAuthPolicyReconciler programmatically constructs AuthPolicy resources with three authentication methods (API key, TokenReview, OIDC JWT) and OPA-based authorization. This is architecturally distinct from the dashboard's kube-auth-proxy OIDC pattern and from the pod-level kube-rbac-proxy pattern used by model-registry.\n126:The relationship between data-science-gateway and maas-default-gateway is unresolved from the structured inventory alone. Both are referenced in the `openshift-ingress` namespace, and they may be the same Gateway resource with different names used by different components, or they may be distinct Gateway resources. The models-as-a-service HTTPRoute explicitly references `maas-default-gateway` as its parentRef.\n133:| maas-default-gateway (MaaS API) | API key (Bearer sk-oai-*), K8s ServiceAccount token, OIDC JWT (optional) | Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy | None observed (Kuadrant operates at policy level) | Authorino: API key callback to maas-api /internal/v1/api-keys/validate; K8s TokenReview; OIDC JWT validation | maas-api TenantAuthMiddleware: TokenReview + SubjectAccessReview for /v1/tenants (internal-only path) | OPA Rego authorization rules for subscription/group/binding; /v1/tenants intentionally excluded from Gateway routing |\n144:| Kuadrant | API authentication and rate limiting | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator |\n153:| kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (SubjectAccessReview) | kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator (monitoring proxies), odh-dashboard, odh-observability | Pod-level TLS-terminating proxy; validates tokens via K8s TokenReview and checks authorization via SubjectAccessReview |\n154:| Kubernetes TokenReview API | kube-auth-proxy, kserve (router), models-as-a-service (maas-api), odh-dashboard (BFF sidecars) | Application-level; validates Bearer tokens against kube-apiserver |\n156:| Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator | Gateway-level; API key, TokenReview, and OIDC JWT authentication with OPA authorization |\n191:| odh-observability | 3 | 0 | 0 | Fail |\n228:| 4 | odh-model-controller | Reconciles InferenceService: adds NetworkPolicy, ServiceMonitor, PodMonitor, Route, Kuadrant AuthPolicy | Kubernetes API |\n235:| 1 | External client | Sends request with API key/token to maas-default-gateway | Gateway (Envoy) |\n236:| 2 | Kuadrant/Authorino | AuthPolicy validates: API key callback to maas-api, TokenReview, optional OIDC JWT; OPA authorizes subscription/group | maas-api |\n237:| 3 | maas-api | Processes model listing, subscription management, or API key lifecycle; queries PostgreSQL for tenant state | PostgreSQL |\n238:| 4 | maas-controller | MaaSModelRefReconciler watches KServe LLMInferenceService for model availability; creates HTTPRoutes for model endpoints | kserve |\n314:| models-as-a-service (maas-api) | Configurable | Stateless API server; state in PostgreSQL |\n348:**Authentication fragmentation.** The platform uses at least four distinct authentication patterns: (1) kube-auth-proxy OIDC at the gateway level for dashboard access, (2) Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy for MaaS API access with API keys, TokenReview, and OIDC, (3) kube-rbac-proxy sidecars for pod-level SubjectAccessReview enforcement on metrics and API endpoints, and (4) application-level TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview in component code. While each pattern serves a valid use case, the lack of a unified authentication architecture means that different capabilities present different credential requirements and failure modes to users and operators.\n350:**Gateway API transition.** The platform is mid-migration from OpenShift Routes to Gateway API. The rhods-operator provisions a central data-science-gateway, but not all components have migrated: model-registry-operator maintains dual ingress, some components still use Routes exclusively, and models-as-a-service references a separately-named gateway (maas-default-gateway). The conditional Istio integration (EnvoyFilter/DestinationRule created only when Istio CRDs are detected) adds complexity to the ingress story, as the authentication chain differs between Istio-present and Istio-absent clusters.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c8604ba3bb4d35d","agent_id":"a228e1c2340ee276c","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"322a73c8-a7e4-4df8-a077-51efa6350ff8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:17:35.370Z","tool