 plan): E001 → E003 → E008 → E010 → E011 = 5.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n  E001[RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 Investigate RHCL/Kuadrant mechanism]\n  E002[RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 Investigate group claim configurability]\n  E004[RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 Helm chart packaging]\n  E005[RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 Metrics instrumentation]\n  E006[RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 maas-api structured logging]\n  E003[RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 OIDC authentication enablement]\n  E007[RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 Perses dashboards + Prometheus]\n  E009[RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 Kuadrant structured logging]\n  E008[RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 CKS/AKS deployment validation]\n  E010[RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 Distributed tracing]\n  E011[RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 Identity flow documentation]\n  E001 --> E003\n  E001 --> E008\n  E001 --> E009\n  E002 --> E011\n  E003 --> E008\n  E004 --> E008\n  E005 --> E007\n  E008 --> E010\n  E001 --> E011\n  E003 --> E011\n  E004 --> E011\n  E005 --> E011\n  E006 --> E011\n  E007 --> E011\n  E008 --> E011\n  E009 --> E011\n  E010 --> E011\n```\n\nBranch edges (not part of the main plan): E001 gates BRANCH-A/B/C-E012; E002 gates BRANCH-A/B-E013. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The Authorino-based OIDC configuration approach is only valid if a Kuadrant deployment path exists; the Envoy ext_authz contingency (outcome C) rewrites this epic. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E008 | Rule 3 | Deployment validation requires a functioning auth/rate-limit layer; which mechanism (RHCL charts, standalone, Envoy) is selected by the investigation. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E009 | Rule 3 | Kuadrant logging configuration depends on the deployment mechanism chosen. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n| E003 → E008 | Rule 12/13 | Deployment validation exercises the OIDC configuration produced by the authentication enablement work. |\n| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 | CKS/AKS deployment is performed via the Helm chart; the chart is the build-against artifact. |\n| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | Perses dashboards are built against the specific metric names/labels emitted by the maas-api instrumentation. |\n| E008 → E010 | Rule 12 | End-to-end tracing (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) can only be validated on a deployed MaaS instance. |\n| all Implementations → E011 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The identity flow documentation describes what was built; blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic plus both Investigations, whose outcomes (deployment mechanism, group claim approach) determine the documented flow. |\n\nParallel-by-design (no edge, per rules 10/14/15): E002, E004, E005, E006 all start immediately; E004 (Helm packaging) is deliberately NOT gated on E002 — the `groupClaimPath` chart value is a configuration reference added with the selected group-claim branch, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 config-reference carve-out). E006 (logging) runs parallel to E005 (metrics): same repo, different work areas, and logging is a deferrable P1 feature with an independent surface (log streams vs. `/metrics` endpoint). No konflux/repo onboarding chains apply: models-as-a-service is an existing in-platform component (architecture context exists), and xKS uses Helm-based installation (OLM/operator onboarding explicitly out of scope).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E004 (chart), E008 (validation), BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (auth/rate-limit layer) |\n| HLR-2 MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E004, E008 |\n| HLR-3 Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | E008 (validation), BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (Limitador/Envoy deployment) |\n| HLR-4 External OIDC authentication validated | P0 | E003, E008 |\n| HLR-5 API key authentication (sk-oai-*) functional on xKS | P0 | E003, E008 |\n| HLR-6 Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E002 (investigation), BRANCH-A-E013 (implement) / BRANCH-B-E013 (fallback documentation) |\n| HLR-7 RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001, BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 |\n| HLR-8 Observability metrics functional | P0 | E005 (instrumentation), E007 (dashboards/Prometheus integration) |\n| HLR-9 Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | BRANCH-A-E013 / BRANCH-B-E013 (claim mapping), E008 (functional validation) |\n| HLR-10 Observability logging functional | P1 | E006 (maas-api), E009 (Authorino/Limitador) |\n| HLR-11 Known limitations documented | P1 | E008 (falls out of validation — incidental, not split) |\n| HLR-12 User identity flow documented for xKS | P2 | E011 |\n| HLR-13 Distributed tracing functional | P2 | E010 |\n\nNo orphaned HLRs. HLR-9 is deliberately bundled with the group-claim work rather than split by priority: tier mapping shares the CEL-predicate/claim-path surface with HLR-6 (redundant-surface rule). HLR-11 stays inside E008 because the limitations list falls out of the validation work with no independent surface.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\n**E001 (RHCL/Kuadrant deployment mechanism)** — bounded, ≤3 outcomes from the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options:\n- **Branch A** — RHCL official xKS support committed, official RHCL Helm charts used → BRANCH-A-E012 (owned by RHCL / OpenShift).\n- **Branch B** — no official support; standalone Authorino + Limitador deployed via Helm → BRANCH-B-E012 (owned by MaaS).\n- **Branch C** — contingency: direct Envoy ext_authz integration without Kuadrant → BRANCH-C-E012 (owned by MaaS). If C is selected, E003's Authorino-based approach is rewritten per its gate_failure_impact.\n\n**E002 (group claim configurability)** — bounded, 2 outcomes:\n- **Branch A** — Tenant CRD extensible post-3.4-GA: add `groupClaimPath` field, pass through to MaaSSubscription CEL predicate generation → BRANCH-A-E013.\n- **Branch B** — Tenant CRD frozen: document the required claim format and push IdP configuration burden to customers → BRANCH-B-E013 (kept as a general Implementation, not `docs-authoring`, so Rule 11 docs-last ordering is not misapplied to a conditional fallback).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **External schedule risk (not an AC)**: MaaS 3.4 GA stable build availability is a critical blocker for the Summit (May 2026) timeline; the stable-build date is an open PM question. Mitigation per strategy: validate against RC builds in parallel; RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo. Recorded as risks on the deployment validation epic, not acceptance criteria.\n2. **Acceptance-criteria contradiction**: \"MaaS successfully deployed on at least on xKS platform (AKS AND CKS)\" mixes \"at least one\" with \"AKS AND CKS\". The HLR list marks both CKS and AKS as P0, so the decomposition requires both; flagged for PM clarification.\n3. **Priority inversion observation**: user identity flow documentation is P2 (HLR-12) although the user-management strategy is one of the two headline solution components and the strategy priority is Blocker. The docs epic inherits P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (frontmatter `priority: Blocker` → P0), but the HLR's P2 label understates its visibility; human verification suggested.\n4. **Scope trap / assumption to verify in-epic**: observability instrumentation feasibility \"without MaaS 3.4 GA code changes\" is unvalidated — if maas-api is frozen, metrics may need a post-GA patch or deferral. Captured as an acceptance criterion on the metrics epic rather than an Investigation (the epic exists and proceeds either way).\n5. **Implementation details intentionally left to the team**: shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL (open question) is a Helm chart value; OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages is covered by the strategy's failure-mode criteria. Neither changes epic structure.\n6. **Human process gates**: the strategy's Prerequisites table lists ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding and License Validation as \"Pending human review\" — reflected as unfavorable `human_process_gates` signals on the Helm packaging epic rather than separate epics (models-as-a-service is an existing in-platform component; no new-repo onboarding chain applies).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe RHCL xKS support dependency has uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n- **Tier 1 (independent work, start immediately)**: E001, E002, E004 (Helm chart packaging), E005 (metrics instrumentation), E006 (structured logging), E007 (dashboards, after E005). All proceed against MaaS 3.4 RC builds and do not depend on the RHCL decision.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003, E008, E009 and the selected BRANCH-*-E012 deployment epic are gated on the E001 outcome; final validation additionally awaits the stable MaaS 3.4 GA build. If RHCL support is not committed (branch B or C), the Dev Preview still delivers via the fallback deployment paths — only the long-term supportability is affected, which the strategy escalates to product leadership rather than blocking on.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":65253,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c8604ba3bb4d35d","agent_id":"a228e1c2340ee276c","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"5403b98f-4231-4e8c-a81b-62dc59cffffb","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:33:11.600Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
