{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-40848811-850c-4ff0-80e8-5a0c89c62b43","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c3690b4ab382c11","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-03pyhi41/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E003.md","content":"# Enable external OIDC authentication for MaaS on xKS\n\n## Description\n\nReplace MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) with external OIDC validation for xKS deployments. Customers provide their own OIDC-compatible identity provider (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, or other standards-compliant providers); Authorino validates OIDC JWTs via its `oidc` identity source configured with the customer's issuer URL and JWKS endpoint. API key authentication (the `sk-oai-*` flow) is already OIDC-agnostic — it uses the maas-api callback validation path — and must remain functional unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Update the MaaS AuthPolicy resources (architecture context: deployment/base/maas-api/policies/auth-policy.yaml) to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source instead of KubernetesTokenReview for xKS deployments, driven by the Tenant CRD's existing external OIDC configuration (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`).\n- Remove OpenShift-specific identity assumptions from the xKS deployment path (TokenReview identity mapping, OpenShift API server audience configuration) while keeping RHOAI behavior unchanged — xKS changes must be additive or configuration-driven (backwards-compatibility NFR).\n- Validate that the API key flow (`sk-oai-*` Bearer tokens, Authorino HTTP callback to maas-api's `/internal/v1/api-keys/validate`) works unchanged on xKS.\n- Coordinate with the RHCL/OpenShift team for OIDC configuration guidance on Authorino (collaboration, not a code deliverable).\n- Implement an OIDC validation failure mode consistent with the strategy: when the OIDC provider is unavailable, API-key requests continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a deployment with a customer-configured OIDC provider, when a user presents a valid OIDC JWT, then authentication succeeds and the identity is available to authorization rules, measured by a successful authenticated request through the gateway.\n- Given the same deployment, when a user presents a valid `sk-oai-*` API key, then the request is authenticated via the maas-api callback path without any OIDC involvement, measured by a successful inference request.\n- Given an OIDC provider outage, when a user authenticates with a valid API key, then the request succeeds; OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error, measured by response status and error body.\n- RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged: the OpenShift TokenReview path still works when deployed on OpenShift, measured by the existing RHOAI authentication flow passing.\n- Configuration is per-deployment (issuer URL, client ID, audience supplied as values), requiring no code changes per customer IdP.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 [P0]: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n- HLR-5 [P0]: API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72824,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c8604ba3bb4d35d","agent_id":"a228e1c2340ee276c","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"a83bd3dc-a574-4fa9-bdf6-516c56512f00","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:33:47.836Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
