{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-942c4623880c6fc3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 9\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E008 acceptance criteria presuppose the auth/rate-limiting layer deployed\n10\t    only by the selected BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 epic (no edge, and branch edges are forbidden),\n11\t    while every BRANCH-*-E012 validation AC presupposes a deployed OIDC-configured\n12\t    MaaS from E003/E004/E008 (branch epics depend only on E001). Mutual artifact dependency\n13\t    discoverable from epic content, captured by no DAG edge; the mechanism-dependent\n14\t    deployment validation that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch,\n15\t    or the branch epics must be scoped to deployment-only with validation deferred\n16\t    to E008.\n17\t- severity: major\n18\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n19\t  description: 'E011 lists Investigations E001 and E002 in dependencies but has gated_by:\n20\t    null, although its documented content is determined by both investigation outcomes\n21\t    (summary: outcomes determine the documented flow; epic scope: per the resolved\n22\t    approach). Set gated_by (action rewrite) on the shaping investigation.'\n23\t- severity: minor\n24\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n25\t  description: E007 live-data ACs (active scrape target for maas-api, dashboard rendering\n26\t    with live data on a CKS-deployed MaaS instance) presuppose a deployed maas-api\n27\t    (E004 chart / E008 deployment) but E007 depends only on E005 — inconsistent with\n28\t    E010, which received an E008 edge for the same validated-on-deployed-instance\n29\t    reason. Capture the validation ordering or scope the final AC to the E008 environment.\n30\t- severity: minor\n31\t  criterion: Completeness\n32\t  description: The strategy PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation (in-memory LRU cache,\n33\t    TTL 5min, for validated API key hashes) is not owned by any epic, and the E008\n34\t    restated PostgreSQL failure-mode AC drops the strategy clause that existing API\n35\t    key validation requests fail on cache miss. Assign the cache mitigation as scope/AC\n36\t    on E003 or E008, or record it as deliberately deferred.\n37\terror: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8003\n40\t\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 9/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 16 epics — consistent. The failures below are content-level: an uncapturable mutual artifact dependency between the main-plan deployment validation and the conditional deployment branches (Criterion 2), and a missing `gated_by` on the docs epic that lists two Investigations in `dependencies` (Criterion 4).\n47\t\n48\t## Criterion Details\n49\t\n50\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n51\tAll 13 HLRs (HLR-1 through HLR-13) map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix is present and matches the epic files' HLR Traceability sections. All P0 HLRs are covered by main-plan or branch epics; P1/P2 HLRs are covered by real epics (not silently deferred). Priority inheritance is correct (validator-confirmed): E001/E002 (P0) gate P0 work; the P1/P2 epics (E006, E009, E010) block only the docs-authoring epic E011, whose Rule 11 edges are exempt from priority inheritance. No problematic priority collapse: HLR-9 (P1) shares the CEL-predicate/claim-path surface with HLR-6 (P0) in the branch E013 epics (redundant-surface rule), and HLR-11 (P1) falls incidentally out of E008's validation work with no independent surface — both bundlings are justified in the summary.\n52\t\n53\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n54\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every edge in the summary DAG (`graph TD`, dependency → dependent) has a matching entry in the target epic's `dependencies` and vice versa; all referenced epics exist; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`; all branch files set both `branch` and `gated_by`; no konflux/license/repo-onboarding chains apply (existing in-platform component, Helm-only installation). Critical path 5 is at the top of the M-range band, accounted for by the Rule 11 docs-last edge.\n55\t\n56\tHowever, there is a **critical** unexpressed data/artifact dependency between the main plan and the conditional deployment branches:\n57\t- E008's acceptance criteria presuppose \"a CKS cluster with the auth/rate-limiting layer and Gateway API (Istio) installed\" — that layer is deployed only by the selected `BRANCH-A/B/C-E012` epic (E001 explicitly scopes the durable deployment out; the branch epics' entire scope is deploying it). E008 has no edge to it, and correctly cannot (branch edges are forbidden) — which means the mechanism-dependent deployment validation, which hinges on a specific branch's work product, belongs inside that branch rather than in a main-plan epic gated only on the Investigation.\n58\t- Conversely, every `BRANCH-*-E012` epic's validation ACs (\"Given a MaaS deployment using this Kuadrant stack, when a user exceeds a configured token rate limit…\" / \"when a user authenticates via OIDC JWT or sk-oai-* API key…\") presuppose a deployed, OIDC-configured MaaS — the output of E003 (AuthPolicy OIDC configuration) and E004 (Helm chart), functionally validated in E008 — yet the branch epics depend only on E001.\n59\t\n60\tThe two sides mutually presuppose each other's artifacts with no edge in either direction; a scheduler reading the frontmatter could run E008 and the selected BRANCH-E012 concurrently and fail E008's first AC. This is discoverable purely from epic content and is a missing-edge critical issue.\n61\t\n62\tMinor: E007's live-data ACs (\"active scrape target for maas-api\", \"dashboard rendering with live data\" on a CKS-deployed MaaS instance) presuppose a deployed maas-api on CKS — a product of E004's chart and effectively E008's deployment — yet E007 depends only on E005. This is inconsistent with E010, which received an E008 edge for exactly the \"can only be validated on a deployed instance\" reason. Either capture the validation ordering or scope E007's final AC to ride on E008's environment.\n63\t\n64\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n65\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: the Maas/xKS-Platform/RHCL-OpenShift splits are correct (E007 Monitoring/xKS Platform; E009 Inference Gateway/RHCL / OpenShift; branch A-E012 owned by RHCL / OpenShift vs. B/C-E012 by MaaS — matching the strategy's ownership of each path). No epic bundles multiple teams. No scope duplication: E006 vs. E009 is a component/team split of the logging requirement (maas-api vs. Authorino/Limitador log sources), and E005 vs. E007 is a producer/consumer split — neither rebuilds the other's surface. Code implementation and content authoring are separated (E011 is pure docs-authoring; E008's limitations documentation is incidental fallout). E008 bundles CKS + AKS validation plus failure modes — same component, same team, same logical change; it sits at the upper bound of ~2 weeks but does not clearly exceed it.\n66\t\n67\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n68\tBoth Investigations are genuine: E001's outcome selects among three bounded deployment mechanisms (changing which branch epics exist and rewriting E003/E008/E009 scope); E002's outcome selects between implementing `groupClaimPath` and the documentation fallback. All `gated_by` fields that are set are accompanied by populated `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach) and reference direct dependencies. Branch files correctly set `branch` and `gated_by`.\n69\t\n70\t**Major issue:** E011 lists both Investigations (E001, E002) in its `dependencies` but has `gated_by: null`. Its own scope states the documented claim-mapping approach is \"per the resolved approach\", and the summary's DAG justification says the Investigations' outcomes \"determine the documented flow\" — i.e., the gate outcome rewrites E011's content. An Investigation dependency without `gated_by` is a rule violation, and automated pipeline consumers detecting gates via the `dependencies` + `gated_by` pairing will miss this one. E011 should set `gated_by` (action `rewrite`) on the investigation that shapes its content.\n71\t\n72\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n73\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (with `ai_signals: null`); all Implementation epics, including the five branch epics, carry `ai_signals`. All 16 ai-signals rationale files exist, and the rationale values match the frontmatter values. Spot-checks against the rubrics hold up: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` (the RHCL commitment does not exist in any readable artifact) and `human_judgment_required: -2` (gating question is a governance decision) are exactly right; E002's `source_accessibility: 1` (answer determined by readable repo/release policy) is right; E004's `human_process_gates: -1` (pending build-onboarding/license gates) and E008's `external_dependency: -1`/`open_questions: -1` are consistent with the strategy. E001's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` (rather than -2) is borderline but the rationale (deployment mechanics exercisable on a local cluster; only CKS-specific confirmation peripheral) is defensible.\n74\t\n75\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n76\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then-style ACs with explicit measurements. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E011 (docs-authoring) has the \"technically reviewed against the implementation\" AC; E003, which replaces the OpenShift identity mechanism, carries the backwards-compatibility AC (\"RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged… TokenReview path still works\"); no konflux chain applies, so Rule 25 is N/A. External schedule constraints are correctly kept out of ACs — the MaaS 3.4 stable-build timing is recorded in Health Warnings and as risk management scope in E008, not as an AC. No bare draft-ID references (\"E001\"-style) appear in any epic body; branch epics reference their gating investigation by name, not ID.\n77\t\n78\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n79\tCore scope is fully accounted for: user-management strategy (E003, E011), CKS+AKS deployment validation with the \"AKS AND CKS\" contradiction flagged for PM clarification (E008 + Health Warnings), rate limiting and subscription/tier management (branches + E008), observability metrics/logging/tracing (E005/E007, E006/E009, E010), Helm packaging with the strategy's enumerated chart values (E004), all four failure-mode criteria (E003 OIDC outage; E008 Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL outage), and the conditional branches cover all bounded outcomes of both investigations (3 + 2). Strategy-level context is carried forward well: risks and assumptions land as ACs (E005 frozen-branch feasibility, E007 Prometheus accessibility) or Health Warnings (3.4 build timing, priority-inversion note), and open questions are resolved into chart values or epic scope.\n80\t\n81\tMinor scope gap: the PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation from the strategy's risk table — \"Implement in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5min) for validated API key hashes\" — is not owned by any epic, and E008's restated PostgreSQL failure-mode AC drops the strategy's \"existing API key validation requests fail (cache miss scenario)\" clause (keeping only API-key-creation failure and continued OIDC auth). The cache-miss behavior the strategy's AC presumes has no implementing epic; capture it as scope/AC on E003 or E008, or note it as a deliberately deferred mitigation.\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b5e1a98d78bdc7","agent_id":"a724c079c1f66f2f0","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"bbfef47f-f2f4-48df-a001-5ec88d4273c7","timestamp":"2