lback demo option.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a CKS cluster with the auth/rate-limiting layer and Gateway API (Istio) installed, when MaaS is deployed via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given an AKS cluster with the same prerequisites, when MaaS is deployed via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n- Given a MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured, when a user exceeds the rate limit, then HTTP 429 is returned, measured by monitoring response status codes.\n- Given a deployment with Limitador terminated, when a user makes an inference request, then it is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and Envoy logs.\n- Given cert-manager-issued TLS certificates are rotated, then active requests continue and new requests succeed, measured by zero connection errors during rotation and successful TLS handshake post-rotation.\n- Given PostgreSQL becomes unavailable, then API key creation fails with HTTP 503 while OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n- A document exists listing all known limitations of MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA, including the engineering delta (code changes, configuration changes, Helm chart packaging).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: MaaS successfully deployed on CKS.\n- HLR-2 [P0]: MaaS successfully deployed on AKS.\n- HLR-3 [P0]: Token rate limiting functional on xKS (functional validation).\n- HLR-4 [P0]: External OIDC authentication validated (end-to-end 