lidation scope: end-to-end authentication enforcement and token rate limiting through the gateway, which depend on the auth/rate-limiting deployment mechanism selected by the investigation and are validated by that mechanism's conditional plan against the MaaS deployment this epic produces.\n- Document known limitations of MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA, and the engineering delta discovered.\n- Manage the MaaS 3.4 build-timing risk: validate against RC builds in parallel while awaiting the stable build; maintain the RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo option.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a CKS cluster with Gateway API (Istio) installed, when MaaS is deployed via the Helm chart with the OIDC configuration values applied, then maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given an AKS cluster, when MaaS is deployed via the Helm chart with the same configuration, then maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when an API key is created, then it is persisted in PostgreSQL and passes validation through maas-api's internal API key validation endpoint, measured by a successful validation response for the created key.\n- Given MaaSSubscription and tier resources, when subscriptions and tiers are created, read, updated, and deleted, then the CRUD operations succeed and tier assignments are reflected in the stored state, measured by API responses and the persisted PostgreSQL state.\n- Given cert-manager-issued TLS certificates are rotated, then active requests continue and new requests succeed, measured by zero connection errors during rotation and successful TLS handshake post-rotation.\n- Given PostgreSQL becomes unavailable, then API key creation fails with HTTP 503, existing API key validation requests fail on cache miss (keys validated within the 5-minute cache TTL continue to succeed), while OIDC token-based authentication is unaffected because OIDC validation does not depend on PostgreSQL, measured by response status and error logs.\n- A document exists listing all known limitations of MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA, including the engineering delta (code changes, configuration changes, Helm chart packaging).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: MaaS successfully deployed on CKS.\n- HLR-2 [P0]: MaaS successfully deployed on AKS.\n- HLR-5 [P0]: API key authentication functional on xKS (persistence and internal validation path; end-to-end enforcement is validated by the selected deployment-mechanism plan).\n- HLR-9 [P1]: Tier/subscription management functional (CRUD validation; claim-based tier mapping is validated by the selected group-claim plan).\n- HLR-11 [P1]: Known limitations documented (falls out of this validation work)."}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":56629,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b5e1a98d78bdc7","agent_id":"a724c079c1f66f2f0","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"701fdd7c-a847-4125-a082-33aef800dcbe","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:54:13.965Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
