{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7447fece0e3d470","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 16\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8003 (RHAII MaaS on xKS)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy is M-sized (3-5 sprints), spans multiple components (MaaS, Kuadrant/RHCL, Gateway API, observability) and three teams (MaaS, xKS Platform, RHCL / OpenShift). Step 0 triage does not apply: neither below-threshold (multi-team, multi-component, effort M) nor docs-only.\n13\t\n14\t**Staff Engineer Input:** the section is empty (template placeholder comments only) — no overrides to log. The strategy body embeds \"per Staff Engineer Input\" attributions (CKS as priority platform; observability as a core value proposition); these are treated as refined strategy content.\n15\t\n16\t**Component name mappings** (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n17\t- `models-as-a-service` (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing, Helm chart packaging) → **Model as a Service**\n18\t- `Kuadrant (Authorino, Limitador)` and the `Gateway API` enforcement layer → **Inference Gateway** (gateway-level authentication/rate-limiting infrastructure; no canonical \"Kuadrant\" component exists)\n19\t- `Observability (Prometheus, Perses)` and CKS Loki integration → **Monitoring**\n20\t\n21\tTeam names are copied verbatim from the strategy's Impacted Teams table: `MaaS`, `xKS Platform`, `RHCL / OpenShift`.\n22\t\n23\t## Epic List\n24\t\n25\tMain plan:\n26\t\n27\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n28\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 | Investigate RHCL/Kuadrant deployment mechanism for MaaS on xKS | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 | Investigate OIDC group claim configurability in the MaaS Tenant CRD | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 | Enable external OIDC authentication for MaaS on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 | Package MaaS components as Helm charts for xKS deployment | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 | Add Prometheus metrics instrumentation to maas-api | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | Add structured logging to maas-api for xKS observability | Implementation | MaaS | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | Deploy Perses dashboards and integrate MaaS metrics with CKS Prometheus | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | Deploy and validate MaaS on CKS and AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | Configure structured logging for Kuadrant auth and rate-limit events on xKS | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P1 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | Add distributed tracing through the MaaS request path | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | Document the user identity flow for MaaS on xKS | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n40\t\n41\tConditional branch epics (not standalone Jira issues; attached to their gating Investigation):\n42\t\n43\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gated by |\n44\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|----------|\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E012 | Deploy Kuadrant on xKS via official RHCL Helm charts | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 | E001 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E012 | Deploy standalone Authorino and Limitador on xKS via Helm | Implementation | MaaS | P0 | E001 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E012 | Integrate direct Envoy ext_authz authentication and rate limiting | Implementation | MaaS | P0 | E001 |\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E013 | Implement configurable groupClaimPath in the Tenant CRD and subscription controller | Implementation | MaaS | P0 | E002 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E013 | Document the required OIDC group claim format for MaaS on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 | E002 |\n50\t\n51\t`epic_count` = 16 (11 main-plan + 5 branch epics). Critical path (main plan): E001 → E003 → E008 → E010 → E011 = 5.\n52\t\n53\t## Dependency DAG\n54\t\n55\t```mermaid\n56\tgraph TD\n57\t  E001[RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 Investigate RHCL/Kuadrant mechanism]\n58\t  E002[RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 Investigate group claim configurability]\n59\t  E004[RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 Helm chart packaging]\n60\t  E005[RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 Metrics instrumentation]\n61\t  E006[RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 maas-api structured logging]\n62\t  E003[RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 OIDC authentication enablement]\n63\t  E007[RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 Perses dashboards + Prometheus]\n64\t  E009[RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 Kuadrant structured logging]\n65\t  E008[RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 CKS/AKS deployment validation]\n66\t  E010[RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 Distributed tracing]\n67\t  E011[RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 Identity flow documentation]\n68\t  E001 --> E003\n69\t  E001 --> E008\n70\t  E001 --> E009\n71\t  E002 --> E011\n72\t  E003 --> E008\n73\t  E004 --> E008\n74\t  E005 --> E007\n75\t  E008 --> E007\n76\t  E008 --> E010\n77\t  E001 --> E011\n78\t  E003 --> E011\n79\t  E004 --> E011\n80\t  E005 --> E011\n81\t  E006 --> E011\n82\t  E007 --> E011\n83\t  E008 --> E011\n84\t  E009 --> E011\n85\t  E010 --> E011\n86\t```\n87\t\n88\tBranch edges (not part of the main plan): E001 gates BRANCH-A/B/C-E012; E002 gates BRANCH-A/B-E013. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic. The BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 epics additionally list E008 in `dependencies`: their end-to-end validation (authentication enforcement, token rate limiting) runs against the MaaS instance deployed and validated there, so the branch→main edge captures the artifact presupposition that previously had no DAG expression (branch→main edges are allowed; only main→branch edges are forbidden).\n89\t\n90\t## DAG Justification\n91\t\n92\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n93\t|------|------|-----------|\n94\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The Authorino-based OIDC configuration approach is only valid if a Kuadrant deployment path exists; the Envoy ext_authz contingency (outcome C) rewrites this epic. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n95\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 3 | The deployment-mechanism outcome rewrites the validation scope: the mechanism-dependent end-to-end validation (auth enforcement, rate limiting) is owned by the selected branch epic, while this epic keeps the mechanism-independent MaaS platform validation. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n96\t| E001 → E009 | Rule 3 | Kuadrant logging configuration depends on the deployment mechanism chosen. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n97\t| E003 → E008 | Rule 12/13 | Deployment validation exercises the OIDC configuration produced by the authentication enablement work. |\n98\t| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 | CKS/AKS deployment is performed via the Helm chart; the chart is the build-against artifact. |\n99\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | Perses dashboards are built against the specific metric names/labels emitted by the maas-api instrumentation. |\n100\t| E008 → E007 | Rule 12 | Live-data dashboard rendering and the active maas-api scrape target can only be validated on a deployed MaaS instance — the same validated-on-deployed-instance rule that orders the tracing epic after deployment validation. |\n101\t| E008 → E010 | Rule 12 | End-to-end tracing (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) can only be validated on a deployed MaaS instance. |\n102\t| all Implementations → E011 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The identity flow documentation describes what was built; blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic plus both Investigations, whose outcomes (deployment mechanism, group claim approach) determine the documented flow. E011 is additionally a true gate on the deployment-mechanism Investigation (`gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`): selecting the Envoy ext_authz contingency rewrites the documented flow. |\n103\t\n104\tParallel-by-design (no edge, per rules 10/14/15): E002, E004, E005, E006 all start immediately; E004 (Helm packaging) is deliberately NOT gated on E002 — the `groupClaimPath` chart value is a configuration reference added with the selected group-claim branch, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 config-reference carve-out). E006 (logging) runs parallel to E005 (metrics): same repo, different work areas, and logging is a deferrable P1 feature with an independent surface (log streams vs. `/metrics` endpoint). No konflux/repo onboarding chains apply: models-as-a-service is an existing in-platform component (architecture context exists), and xKS uses Helm-based installation (OLM/operator onboarding explicitly out of scope).\n105\t\n106\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n107\t\n108\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n109\t|-----|----------|---------|\n110\t| HLR-1 MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E004 (chart), E008 (validation), BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (auth/rate-limit layer) |\n111\t| HLR-2 MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E004, E008 |\n112\t| HLR-3 Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (mechanism deployment + end-to-end validation) |\n113\t| HLR-4 External OIDC authentication validated | P0 | E003 (configuration), BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (end-to-end validation) |\n114\t| HLR-5 API key authentication (sk-oai-*) functional on xKS | P0 | E003 (validation path + PG-outage cache), E008 (persistence/internal validation), BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 (end-to-end) |\n115\t| HLR-6 Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E002 (investigation), BRANCH-A-E013 (implement) / BRANCH-B-E013 (fallback documentation) |\n116\t| HLR-7 RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001, BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 |\n117\t| HLR-8 Observability metrics functional | P0 | E005 (instrumentation), E007 (dashboards/Prometheus integration) |\n118\t| HLR-9 Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | BRANCH-A-E013 / BRANCH-B-E013 (claim mapping), E008 (subscription/tier CRUD validation) |\n119\t| HLR-10 Observability logging functional | P1 | E006 (maas-api), E009 (Authorino/Limitador) |\n120\t| HLR-11 Known limitations documented | P1 | E008 (falls out of validation — incidental, not split) |\n121\t| HLR-12 User identity flow documented for xKS | P2 | E011 |\n122\t| HLR-13 Distributed tracing functional | P2 | E010 |\n123\t\n124\tNo orphaned HLRs. HLR-9 is deliberately bundled with the group-claim work rather than split by priority: tier mapping shares the CEL-predicate/claim-path surface with HLR-6 (redundant-surface rule). HLR-11 stays inside E008 because the limitations list falls out of the validation work with no independent surface.\n125\t\n126\t## Conditional Branches\n127\t\n128\t**E001 (RHCL/Kuadrant deployment mechanism)** — bounded, ≤3 outcomes from the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options. Each branch epic deploys the auth/rate-limiting layer and owns the mechanism-dependent end-to-end validation (authentication enforcement, token rate limiting, enforcement-layer failure mode) against the MaaS instance deployed and validated by the main-plan deployment validation epic:\n129\t- **Branch A** — RHCL official xKS support committed, official RHCL Helm charts used → BRANCH-A-E012 (owned by RHCL / OpenShift).\n130\t- **Branch B** — no official support; standalone Authorino + Limitador deployed via Helm → BRANCH-B-E012 (owned by MaaS).\n131\t- **Branch C** — contingency: direct Envoy ext_authz integration without Kuadrant → BRANCH-C-E012 (owned by MaaS). If C is selected, E003's Authorino-based approach is rewritten per its gate_failure_impact.\n132\t\n133\t**E002 (group claim configurability)** — bounded, 2 outcomes:\n134\t- **Branch A** — Tenant CRD extensible post-3.4-GA: add `groupClaimPath` field, pass through to MaaSSubscription CEL predicate generation → BRANCH-A-E013.\n135\t- **Branch B** — Tenant CRD frozen: document the required claim format and push IdP configuration burden to customers → BRANCH-B-E013 (kept as a general Implementation, not `docs-authoring`, so Rule 11 docs-last ordering is not misapplied to a conditional fallback).\n136\t\n137\t## Health Warnings\n138\t\n139\t1. **External schedule risk (not an AC)**: MaaS 3.4 GA stable build availability is a critical blocker for the Summit (May 2026) timeline; the stable-build date is an open PM question. Mitigation per strategy: validate against RC builds in parallel; RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo. Recorded as risks on the deployment validation epic, not acceptance criteria.\n140\t2. **Acceptance-criteria contradiction**: \"MaaS successfully deployed on at least on xKS platform (AKS AND CKS)\" mixes \"at least one\" with \"AKS AND CKS\". The HLR list marks both CKS and AKS as P0, so the decomposition requires both; flagged for PM clarification.\n141\t3. **Priority inversion observation**: user identity flow documentation is P2 (HLR-12) although the user-management strategy is one of the two headline solution components and the strategy priority is Blocker. The docs epic inherits P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (frontmatter `priority: Blocker` → P0), but the HLR's P2 label understates its visibility; human verification suggested.\n142\t4. **Scope trap / assumption to verify in-epic**: observability instrumentation feasibility \"without MaaS 3.4 GA code changes\" is unvalidated — if maas-api is frozen, metrics may need a post-GA patch or deferral. Captured as an acceptance criterion on the metrics epic rather than an Investigation (the epic exists and proceeds either way).\n143\t5. **Implementation details intentionally left to the team**: shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL (open question) is a Helm chart value; OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages is covered by the strategy's failure-mode criteria. Neither changes epic structure.\n144\t6. **Human process gates**: the strategy's Prerequisites table lists ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding and License Validation as \"Pending human review\" — reflected as unfavorable `human_process_gates` signals on the Helm packaging epic rather than separate epics (models-as-a-service is an existing in-platform component; no new-repo onboarding chain applies).\n145\t\n146\t## Tiered Delivery\n147\t\n148\tThe RHCL xKS support dependency has uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n149\t- **Tier 1 (independent work, start immediately)**: E001, E002, E004 (Helm chart packaging), E005 (metrics instrumentation), E006 (structured logging), E007 (dashboards, after E005). All proceed against MaaS 3.4 RC builds and do not depend on the RHCL decision.\n150\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003, E008, E009 and the selected BRANCH-*-E012 deployment epic are gated on the E001 outcome; the selected branch epic's end-to-end validation runs against the MaaS deployment validated by E008 (branch → E008 edge); final validation additionally awaits the stable MaaS 3.4 GA build. If RHCL support is not committed (branch B or C), the Dev Preview still delivers via the fallback deployment paths — only the long-term supportability is affected, which the strategy escalates to product leadership rather than blocking on.\n151\t\n152\t## Revision Notes (post-review)\n153\t\n154\t1. **Mechanism-dependent validation moved into the deployment-mechanism branches** (critical DAG finding): E008 no longer presupposes the auth/rate-limiting layer deployed by the selected branch — its scope and ACs now cover only mechanism-independent MaaS platform validation (Helm deployment health on CKS/AKS, subscription/tier CRUD, API key persistence and internal validation, cert rotation, PostgreSQL failure mode, limitations document). The end-to-end authentication/rate-limiting validation and the Limitador-termination (or ext_authz-unavailability) failure mode moved into BRANCH-A/B/C-E012, which additionally list E008 in `dependencies` so their \"Given a MaaS deployment using this stack\" ACs rest on a real DAG edge (branch → main edges are permitted; main → branch edges are not). HLR-3/4/5 traceability updated accordingly.\n155\t2. **Docs epic gate recorded**: E011 sets `gated_by=E001` (action `rewrite`) — the deployment-mechanism outcome shapes the documented identity flow (Envoy ext_authz would rewrite it); previously the Investigations were listed in `dependencies` with no gate recorded.\n156\t3. **Dashboard/validation ordering captured**: E007 adds an E008 dependency — its live-data dashboard and active-scrape-target ACs can only be validated on a deployed MaaS instance (same Rule 12 reason as the tracing epic). Critical path unchanged at 5 (E001 → E003 → E008 → E007/E010 → E011).\n157\t4. **PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation owned**: the strategy's in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5 min) for validated API key hashes is now scope plus an AC on E003 (owner of the API key validation path), and E008's PostgreSQL failure-mode AC restates the strategy's cache-miss clause (existing API key validation requests fail on cache miss; keys validated within the TTL continue to succeed).\n158\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-951f52331839b74d","agent_id":"a2f970ffe7fafef2b","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"d99d1457-d2ba-4d3e-8d91-63d17d7f81e8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:57:06.889Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7447fece0e3d470","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
