uns last and is technically reviewed against the implementation.\n50\t\n51\t## Scope\n52\t\n53\t- Content outline:\n54\t  - User management strategy for xKS: why external OIDC replaces Kubernetes RBAC identity mapping, and what customers must provide (OIDC-compatible identity provider).\n55\t  - User identity flow: customer IdP → OIDC JWT → gateway/Authorino validation → identity injection → subscription/tier selection → API key issuance and use.\n56\t  - Identity-to-subscription mapping: how OIDC group claims map to tiers, including the group claim format supported by the delivered configuration (configurable claim path or documented required format, per the resolved approach).\n57\t  - Known limitations of the Dev Preview versus RHOAI MaaS GA, cross-referenced with the limitations identified during deployment validation.\n58\t  - Failure modes from the user's perspective (IdP outage behavior, API-key vs OIDC dependence).\n59\t- Publish through the Red Hat docs content process.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- A published document exists describing the user authentication strategy and the user identity flow for xKS deployments, covering the outline above.\n64\t- The documented flow matches the implemented behavior (group claim mapping, OIDC and API key paths, failure modes), measured by technical review against the deployed MaaS instance on CKS.\n65\t- The document states what users must do (provide an OIDC-compatible identity provider) and the supported/required claim formats.\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability\n68\t\n69\t- HLR-12 [P2]: User identity flow documented for xKS deployments.\n70\t- Strategy acceptance criteria (User Management): user authentication strategy defined and documented; user identity flow documented.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-951f52331839b74d","agent_id":"a2f970ffe7fafef2b","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"b46c98bb-4457-4ff5-bad4-f53f930f480b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:57:29.188Z","to