l path 5 is within the M-heuristic range given the new-platform packaging surface.\n73\t\n74\tHowever, two critical missing-edge findings (data/artifact dependencies discoverable from epic content, not captured in the DAG):\n75\t\n76\t1. **BRANCH-A-E013 and BRANCH-B-E013 validation ACs presuppose deployed artifacts with no DAG edge.** BRANCH-B-E013's scope explicitly says \"Verify the documented guidance against at least one real identity provider configuration on **the CKS validation deployment**\" — E008's artifact — and both branches' tier-mapping ACs are \"measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement,\" which requires the enforcement layer deployed by the selected BRANCH-*-E012. Both E013 branches list only E002 in `dependencies`. Branch → main edges are permitted (the E012 branches carry exactly this E008 edge for exactly this reason, per Revision Note 1), so the omission is a missing edge, not a structural impossibility. The revision fixed the E012 branches but not the E013 branches.\n77\t\n78\t2. **E010's end-to-end trace AC hinges on branch-specific work but lives in a main-plan epic.** Its AC requires \"a distributed trace exists covering gateway ingress, Authorino authentication, Limitador rate limiting, payload-processing, and the model backend,\" and its scope propagates trace context through \"gateway → Authorino → Limitador → …\". The enforcement layer is deployed by the selected BRANCH-*-E012 epic — E008's scope explicitly excludes it — and E010 (deps: E008 only) is unordered with respect to the selected E012 branch. Since main → branch edges are forbidden, work that hinges on a specific branch's outcome belongs inside that branch (or must hinge on the Investigation epic itself with a recorded gate): the enforcement-segment trace validation belongs in the E012 branches, or E010 must be gated on E001 with the traced segments following the selected mechanism.\n79\t\n80\tEither finding alone is critical per the missing-edge rule; together they zero this criterion.\n81\t\n82\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n83\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics correctly: E001/E009 (Inference Gateway) are split by team (MaaS investigation vs RHCL/OpenShift configuration); E005/E007 split instrumentation (Model as a Service, MaaS) from dashboards/Prometheus integration (Monitoring, xKS Platform); E006/E009 split maas-api logging from Kuadrant logging by component and team. No epic bundles multiple teams. No scope duplication: the E005/E007 and E006/E009 pairs build different surfaces (instrumentation vs dashboards; maas-api log streams vs Kuadrant log configuration) — incidental overlap only (shared metric/log field names). No work-product type mixing: E008's known-limitations document is a validation output falling out of the validation work (strategy AC), not bundled content authoring; E011 is the dedicated docs-authoring epic. Epic sizes are within a sprint or two each; E008 (two platforms + validation + limitations doc) is the largest but is the strategy's core validation deliverable and plausibly single-sprint-per-platform. BRANCH-B-E013 being pure documentation typed as general Implementation is a deliberate, documented choice (summary explains why docs-authoring/Rule 11 is not applied to a conditional fallback) — defensible.\n84\t\n85\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n86\tBoth Investigations (E001, E002) genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes which downstream epics exist (the five branch epics), and both have downstream gates. Every epic with non-null `gated_by` has a `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated (E003, E008, E009, E011, all five branches). Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E003/E008/E009/E011 → E001; BRANCH-*-E012 → E001; BRANCH-*-E013 → E002). Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. One minor observation: E011 lists Investigation E002 in `dependencies` without a gate on it — defensible, because E011's scope text pre-adapts to either E002 outcome (\"configurable claim path or documented required format, per the resolved approach\"), making it an absorbed content/scheduling dependency rather than a true gate, and `gated_by` is scalar (already used for the stronger E001 rewrite); but neither the epic nor the summary explicitly justifies leaving the E002 dependency ungated.\n87\t\n88\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n89\tType-appropriate signal fields are used everywhere (E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` with `ai_signals: null`; all Implementation epics including the docs-authoring E011 and all five branch epics carry `ai_signals`), and all 16 ai-signals rationale files exist with values matching the frontmatter. Most values are well-justified (e.g., E001 `source_accessibility: 0` correctly applies the \"answer doesn't exist yet in a readable artifact\" condition to the pending RHCL commitment; E008 `external_dependency: -1` for cluster/stable-build/RHCL coordination; E004 `human_process_gates: -1` for the pending build-onboarding/license gates). Two arguable values: (a) E001 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` — the investigation's third question (\"Validate Kuadrant installation on a CKS test environment with the Istio GatewayClass\") is one of the outcome-selecting questions and needs a live cluster, which leans -2 under the \"gating question needs a live cluster\" condition; the -1 rationale (local cluster answers the mechanics; CKS specifics peripheral) is coherent but borderline. (b) E003 `open_questions: 0` — the strategy resolves the approach and specifies the caching mitigation, so +1 is arguable; 0 as a conservative neutral is defensible. Neither contradicts the rubric outright.\n90\t\n91\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n92\tEvery epic has testable, mostly Given/When/Then acceptance criteria with explicit measurements derived from the strategy's own happy-path and failure-mode criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E003 (a replacement of the identity mechanism) carries the backwards-compatibility AC (\"RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged… TokenReview path still works\"); E011 (docs-authoring) carries the technical-review-against-implementation AC; no konflux chain exists so the build-pipeline AC is N/A. The strategy's external schedule risk (MaaS 3.4 stable-build timing) is correctly kept out of ACs — recorded as epic risk and Health Warning 1 — and no AC states a lab/board/schedule constraint. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by grep; all ID mentions are frontmatter). One minor nit: E004's fifth AC (\"The chart is structured for integration into the RHAII xKS Helm-based installation path (reviewed with the xKS Platform team)\") is softer than the rest — \"structured for integration\" is not sharply testable, though the team-review clause gives it a verification hook.\n93\t\n94\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n95\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: user-management strategy and identity flow (E003, E011, E002 + branches), CKS/AKS deployment validation (E004, E008), token rate limiting and API access control (BRANCH-*-E012 enforcement ACs), tier/subscription management (E008 CRUD + branch claim mapping), API key flow including the PostgreSQL-outage LRU cache (E003 + E008), all four failure-mode criteria (OIDC outage → E003; Limitador termination → E012 branches; cert rotation → E008; PG unavailability → E003/E008), observability metrics/logging/tracing (E005/E006/E007/E009/E010), and known-limitations documentation (E008 + BRANCH-C-E012 delta record). Conditional branches cover all bounded outcomes: E001's three outcomes match the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options (RHCL charts / standalone / Envoy ext_authz); E002's two outcomes match the groupClaimPath/frozen-CRD fallback. Strategy-level context is carried forward: every strategy risk and \"needs validation\" assumption maps to an epic AC, scope item, or health warning (build timing, RHCL escalation, Prometheus/Lok