{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99fabea1fa1dd468","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n3\ttitle: Enable external OIDC authentication for MaaS on xKS\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency is selected for the\n15\t    auth/rate-limiting layer, the OIDC enforcement configuration is rewritten to target\n16\t    Envoy ext_authz instead of Authorino AuthPolicy resources.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 1\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: 0\n23\t  external_dependency: 0\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 5\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Enable external OIDC authentication for MaaS on xKS\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tReplace MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) with external OIDC validation for xKS deployments. Customers provide their own OIDC-compatible identity provider (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, or other standards-compliant providers); Authorino validates OIDC JWTs via its `oidc` identity source configured with the customer's issuer URL and JWKS endpoint. API key authentication (the `sk-oai-*` flow) is already OIDC-agnostic — it uses the maas-api callback validation path — and must remain functional unchanged.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Update the MaaS AuthPolicy resources (architecture context: deployment/base/maas-api/policies/auth-policy.yaml) to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source instead of KubernetesTokenReview for xKS deployments, driven by the Tenant CRD's existing external OIDC configuration (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`).\n43\t- Remove OpenShift-specific identity assumptions from the xKS deployment path (TokenReview identity mapping, OpenShift API server audience configuration) while keeping RHOAI behavior unchanged — xKS changes must be additive or configuration-driven (backwards-compatibility NFR).\n44\t- Validate that the API key flow (`sk-oai-*` Bearer tokens, Authorino HTTP callback to maas-api's `/internal/v1/api-keys/validate`) works unchanged on xKS.\n45\t- Coordinate with the RHCL/OpenShift team for OIDC configuration guidance on Authorino (collaboration, not a code deliverable).\n46\t- Implement an OIDC validation failure mode consistent with the strategy: when the OIDC provider is unavailable, API-key requests continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error.\n47\t- Implement the strategy's PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation in the API key validation path: an in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 minutes) for validated API key hashes, so that recently validated API keys continue to authenticate during a PostgreSQL outage while cache-miss validations fail.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- Given a deployment with a customer-configured OIDC provider, when a user presents a valid OIDC JWT, then authentication succeeds and the identity is available to authorization rules, measured by a successful authenticated request through the gateway.\n52\t- Given the same deployment, when a user presents a valid `sk-oai-*` API key, then the request is authenticated via the maas-api callback path without any OIDC involvement, measured by a successful inference request.\n53\t- Given an OIDC provider outage, when a user authenticates with a valid API key, then the request succeeds; OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error, measured by response status and error body.\n54\t- Given a PostgreSQL outage, when a request presents an API key validated within the last 5 minutes, then validation succeeds from the in-memory cache; when a request presents an API key not in the cache (cache miss), then validation fails, measured by response status codes during an induced PostgreSQL outage.\n55\t- RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged: the OpenShift TokenReview path still works when deployed on OpenShift, measured by the existing RHOAI authentication flow passing.\n56\t- Configuration is per-deployment (issuer URL, client ID, audience supplied as values), requiring no code changes per customer IdP.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- HLR-4 [P0]: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n61\t- HLR-5 [P0]: API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ace5ef834c7ebcc","agent_id":"aaf3692cfeb50acdd","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"98ce8761-c745-41a6-adb9-faefb1a0ddbc","timestamp":"2026-10-22T08:07:30.399Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99fabea1fa1dd468","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
