deferred. Priority inheritance is clean: E001/E002 are P0 Investigations gating P0 work; E011 (docs-authoring, P0) derives from the strategy's Blocker priority per the docs-authoring exemption, and its incoming Rule 11 edges from P1 (E006, E009) and P2 (E010) implementations are exempt. The E008 bundling of P1 HLR-9 CRUD validation with the P0 deployment validation is incidental to validating the deployed instance (the group-claim portion of HLR-9 lives in the branch epics), and HLR-11 falls out of the validation work — neither is a deferrable feature collapse. Minor observation only.\n62\t\n63\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n64\tChecked and passed: diagram/frontmatter cross-consistency (all 19 main-plan edges in the `graph TD` diagram match the target epics' `dependencies` lists exactly, arrow direction correct, no extras in either direction); no circular dependencies; every `dependencies` target exists; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic (the E010 and E011 gating on E001 in place of forbidden main→branch edges is correctly done); all five branch files set both `branch` and `gated_by`; every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies`; no konflux/repo-onboarding chains exist (all `implementation_type` null except E011's docs-authoring), so rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not implicated; branch→main E008 edges are permitted and justified.\n65\t\n66\t**Critical issue — missing edge E008 → E006.** E006 (maas-api structured logging) has `dependencies: []`, yet its first two acceptance criteria are \"Given a deployed MaaS instance with structured logging enabled … measured by querying Grafana Loki with label filters\" — validation that, by the decomposition's own stated rule, \"can only be [exercised] on a deployed MaaS instance.\" That exact rule was applied to add E008 dependencies to E007 (revision note 3: live-data dashboard and active scrape target) and E010 (revision note 6 / DAG justification: end-to-end tracing), and the E006 scope explicitly targets \"the CKS Grafana Loki infrastructure.\" E006's Loki-queried ACs consume the same E008 artifact (the validated CKS deployment) that E007's Prometheus-queried ACs do; the edge is discoverable directly from E006's epic content and is absent from both the diagram and the frontmatter. The parallel-by-design note only justifies E006's parallelism with E005 (same repo, different work areas), which is not inconsistent with an E008 edge — E005 has none because its ACs are satisfiable against \"a running maas-api,\" not the CKS validation deployment. Per the missing-edge rule this is critical and forces this criterion to 0. (Note: the E011 → E002 ungated Investigation dependency and the E006 parallelism are treated under Type Correctness and Epic Boundaries respectively.)\n67\t\n68\tCritical path 5 (E001 → E003 → E008 → E007/E010 → E011) is one above the M-standard heuristic (3-4), but the length is driven by the Rule 11 docs-last edge set plus the E001 mechanism gate, both rule-mandated — not flagged.\n69\t\n70\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n71\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: Model as a Service/MaaS (E002-E006, E008, E010, E011, BRANCH-B/C-E012, BRANCH-A/B-E013), Monitoring/xKS Platform (E007), Inference Gateway/RHCL / OpenShift (E009, BRANCH-A-E012). No epic bundles multiple components or teams. The maas-api vs Kuadrant logging split (E006 vs E009) and the instrumentation vs dashboards split (E005 vs E007) are correct axes, not duplication — complementary surfaces, different owners. BRANCH-B-E013 is pure documentation (no code/content mixing within a single epic). E008 (CKS + AKS deployment validation plus limitations documentation) is the largest epic but is one logical change (same chart, same procedure, two platforms) and the limitations doc falls out of the validation — within sprint scale for an M strategy of 3-5 sprints.\n72\t\n73\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n74\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 selects among three bounded deployment mechanisms (rewriting E003/E008/E009/E010/E011 per their `gate_failure_impact`), E002 selects between two group-claim outcomes. Every `gated_by` (E003, E008, E009, E010, E011, all five branch epics) has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every gate target is in the epic's direct `dependencies`. All Implementations produce concrete artifacts. One noted deviation, accepted as justified: E011 lists the Investigation E002 in `dependencies` without gating on it. The rule's premise (\"by definition the Investigation outcome changes the downstream epic's scope or existence\") does not hold here — E011's identity-to-subscription section pre-adapts to either outcome with unchanged scope, the scalar `gated_by` slot is reserved for the mechanism Investigation whose contingency genuinely rewrites the epic, and the deviation is explicitly documented in both the epic body and the DAG justification. Recorded as a minor issue for the record, not a scoring deduction.\n75\t\n76\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n77\tType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout (Investigations carry `investigation_signals`, Implementations carry `ai_signals`); all 17 ai-signals files exist and their rationale tables match the frontmatter values; E001's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` and E003's `open_questions: +1` re-scores are consistent with their rationales. Two arguable values, both on the observability instrumentation epics: (a) E005 `open_questions: 0` — the strategy's assumption table marks \"observability instrumentation in maas-api does not require MaaS 3.4 GA code changes\" as Needs validation, and the epic's own scope branches on it (direct delivery vs post-3.4 patch); the defined fallback makes 0 defensible, but -1 (approach-changing unknown) is at least as accurate. (b) E006 `open_questions: 1` and `external_dependency: 0` — the epic's own ACs are measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki, whose accessibility for MaaS is a strategy assumption marked Needs validation and owned by no epic (see Criterion 7); claiming all design questions resolved while acceptance depends on an unvalidated external assumption is borderline, and unlike the MaaS-team-adjacent E007 case, E006 is a MaaS-team epic dependent on xKS/CoreWeave infrastructure.\n78\t\n79\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n80\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then ACs derived from the strategy's own acceptance criteria, including the failure modes (OIDC outage, Limitador termination / ext_authz unavailability, cert rotation, PostgreSQL outage with the LRU-cache TTL clause). Rule-mandated ACs are present: E003 (a replacement of the TokenReview path) carries the backwards-compatibility AC (\"RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged … TokenReview path still works\"); E011 (docs-authoring) carries the technical-review-against-implementation AC; no konflux chain applies. No external-schedule constraints are misfiled as ACs — the MaaS 3.4 build-timing risk is correctly recorded in E008 scope/risks and the summary's Health Warnings, not as an AC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (all cross-references use full epic IDs or descriptive phrases). One minor: BRANCH-A-E012's AC \"The RHCL xKS support terms … are documented, measured by a written statement from the RHCL/OpenShift team\" makes an external team's deliverable the measurement of an AC — the engineering-testable portion is the documentation of terms; the written statement itself is a risk/coordination item. Two or fewer minors in this criterion, so no point deduction.\n81\t\n82\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n83\tFull strategy scope is accounted for: user management strategy (E003, E011, BRANCH-A/B-E013), CKS+AKS deployment validation (E004, E008), all four included Dev Preview features (rate limiting, tier/subscription management, external OIDC, API access control), all three observability tiers (E005+E007, E006+E009, E010), known-limitations documentation (E008), all four failure-mode criteria (E003, E008, BRANCH-*-E012), and the PostgreSQL LRU-cache mitigation (E003, restated in E008). Exclusions (operator install, dashboard UI, production-readiness, EKS/GKE) correctly absent. Conditional branches cover all bounded outcomes: E001's three outcomes match the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options exactly; E002's two outcomes match the primary/fallback claim-path approaches. Strategy-level context is carried forward (build-timing risk, RHCL escalation path, feasibility assumptions as ACs, shared-vs-dedicated PostgreSQL as a chart value). A docs-authoring epic exists (E011) despite the Prerequisites table listing Documentation Support as \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\" — required regardless by HLR-12 and the strategy's user-management acceptance criteria. One minor gap: the strategy assumption \"CKS provides Prometheus and Grafana Loki infrastructure\" is split — the Prometheus half has an explicit confirm-or-escalate AC on E007, but the Loki half has no equivalent anywhere; E006's ACs would simply fail if the assumption is false, with no confirmation/escalation path recorded.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-949830f290ee01a8","agent_id":"ab0f9faf32e1f9a83","session_id":"50e38564-4e5e-45db-b1e4-649369677e1e","uuid":"fca38eb3-9c18-489d-9941-f490c4d4c327","timestamp":"2026-10-22T08:19:49.924Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9098e48879a85b89","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Final revise RHAISTRAT-8003"}
